    "q-252030": {
      "dom_id": "step-q252030",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Let the random variable $Y$ be defined as\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{9}^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le 9.$ Find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\le y)=0.90.$\n\n*Hint: The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X∼{χ}^{2}(k).$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,23.209\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |\n| $11$ | $\\,24.725\\,$ | $\\,19.675\\,$ | $\\,17.275\\,$ | $\\,15.767\\,$ | $\\,14.631\\,$ |\n\nThe value of $y$ that satisfies $P(Y\\le y)=0.90$ is $y=$\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "If ${Z}_{i}∼N(0,1)$ for $1\\le i\\le k$ are independent normal random variables, then the sum\n\n$$\n{Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{k}^{2}\n$$\n\nfollows a ${χ}^{2}(k)$ distribution with $k$ degrees of freedom.\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\nY={Z}_{1}^{2}+{Z}_{2}^{2}+⋯+{Z}_{9}^{2}∼{χ}^{2}(9).\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the given probability in the form $P(Y\\ge y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le y) & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ 0.90 & =1-P(Y\\ge y) \\\\ P(Y\\ge y) & =0.10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we wish to find the value $y$ such that $P(Y\\ge y)=0.10$ This can be found from the given table:\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.010$ | $0.050$ | $0.100$ | $0.150$ | $0.200$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $9$ | $\\,21.666\\,$ | $\\,16.919\\,$ | $\\,14.684\\,$ | $\\,13.288\\,$ | $\\,12.242\\,$ |\n| $10$ | $\\,23.209\\,$ | $\\,18.307\\,$ | $\\,15.987\\,$ | $\\,14.534\\,$ | $\\,13.442\\,$ |\n| $11$ | $\\,24.725\\,$ | $\\,19.675\\,$ | $\\,17.275\\,$ | $\\,15.767\\,$ | $\\,14.631\\,$ |\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 14.684)=0.10\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 14.684)=0.90.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=14.684.$",
